<!-- Maven uses repositories to store and retrieve project dependencies and build artifacts.

1. Local repository: A directory on the developer's computer where Maven caches downloaded dependencies and locally 
installed artifacts. Its default location is usually .m2/repository inside the user's home directory.

2. Central repository: The default public Maven repository that provides many Java libraries and plugins. Maven can 
download dependencies from it when they are not already available locally.

3. Remote repository: A repository hosted on another server, such as a company's private repository or another public 
repository. It can be configured in Maven when a project needs artifacts that are not available from the repositories 
already configured.

Technically, Maven Central is itself a remote repository. In this comparison, the term “remote repository” usually 
means an additional repository configured for a specific project or organization. -->

<repositories>
    <repository>
        <id>company-repo</id>
        <url>https://repo.example.com/maven2</url>
    </repository>
</repositories>